1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,160
Mould grows when humidity stays high.

2
00:00:03,510 --> 00:00:13,130
For Singapore heritage collections, prevention means keeping rooms below about 65 per cent relative humidity, nights and weekends included.

3
00:00:13,630 --> 00:00:16,140
Outdoor air here is well above that.

4
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:25,210
Official figures put Singapore's 24-hour mean relative humidity between 76 and 82.6 per cent in every year from 2016 to 2023.

5
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:32,330
The Canadian Conservation Institute says that below 65 per cent, mould will not grow at any temperature.

6
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:36,500
At 85 per cent, it will probably appear in less than a week.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:47,400
Log humidity around the clock, keep boxes off floors and external walls, watch when the air-conditioning switches off, and quarantine new arrivals before they enter the store.

8
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:53,440
If you find mould, wear protective equipment and dry it out before any cleaning.

9
00:00:53,790 --> 00:00:59,050
Then clean gently with a HEPA vacuum and soft brushes, and avoid fungicides.

10
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:03,350
Staining stays, so stabilised items are worth digitising.

11
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:11,280
Micrographics Data provides book and slide scanning, and acid-free boxing, for heritage collections in Singapore.

12
00:01:11,630 --> 00:01:14,590
Request a quote at micrographicsdata.com.
